\section{Give the Timing Graph an External Contract}

Static timing analysis (STA) computes arrival and required times on paths without enumerating input vectors. The graph includes cell arcs and interconnect; its endpoints include register data pins and constrained ports. Clock definitions decide which launch and capture edges to compare. A Liberty library provides characterized arcs; extracted interconnect such as SPEF supplies resistance and capacitance after routing. Earlier estimates can guide synthesis, but do not substitute for checking the routed interconnect and relevant process, voltage, and temperature corners.

Input and output delays model the external paths relative to a reference clock. A port with no delay constraint is not thereby promised to have zero off-chip delay. A generated clock related to the original should describe the hardware's real edge relationship, not be replaced by an arbitrary independent clock of the same nominal frequency. Audit unconstrained endpoints and inspect which paths an exception changes. A fast report from a partly unconstrained design is not timing closure.

