\section{Check Both Sides of the Capture Window}

Suppose \(T=5.00\) ns, with clock arrivals at the launch and capture registers of \(0.15\) ns and \(0.25\) ns relative to their nominal edges. For setup the max clock-to-Q is \(0.35\) ns, max data path is \(4.40\) ns, setup is \(0.20\) ns, and setup uncertainty is \(0.20\) ns. For hold take min clock-to-Q and data path to be \(0.10\) and \(0.12\) ns, hold time \(0.12\) ns, and hold uncertainty \(0.03\) ns. These are illustrative numbers, not characterization. \Cref{fig:visual-318} shows the timing requirements that the same data path has to meet.

Pseudocode for the simple same-clock check, omitting effects such as common-path pessimism removal:

\begin{verbatim}
latest = launch + tcq_max + path_max
setup_by = period + capture - setup - setup_uncertainty
setup_slack = setup_by - latest      # soon enough for next edge?

earliest = launch + tcq_min + path_min
hold_until = capture + hold + hold_uncertainty
hold_slack = earliest - hold_until   # late enough for current edge?
\end{verbatim}

The late arrival is \(4.90\) ns and its deadline \(4.85\) ns, for setup slack of \(-0.05\) ns. The early arrival is \(0.37\) ns and must wait until \(0.40\) ns, for hold slack of \(-0.03\) ns. Increasing the period by \(0.05\) ns would fix setup only in this simplified model; it does not change this same-edge hold check. Delaying the capture clock helps setup but worsens hold, while adding delay to the data path has the opposite tradeoff.

